", \"timestamp\": 1792633075.9559379, \"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"call_RNxhhK88Oy5Z7ySdDCUFx8oL\", \"call_id\": \"call_RNxhhK88Oy5Z7ySdDCUFx8oL\", \"response_item_id\": \"fc_RNxhhK88Oy5Z7ySdDCUFx8oL\", \"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": \"skill_view\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"arc-demo-budget-strategy\\\",\\\"file_path\\\":\\\"\\\"}\"}}]}, {\"id\": 8298, \"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": \"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"name\\\": \\\"arc-demo-budget-strategy\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Use when choosing whether to request ARC demos.\\\", \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"arc\\\", \\\"demonstrations\\\", \\\"information-gain\\\"], \\\"related_skills\\\": [\\\"arc-grid-reasoning\\\", \\\"arc-demo-hypothesis-audit\\\"], \\\"content\\\": \\\"---\\\\nname: arc-demo-budget-strategy\\\\ndescription: Use when choosing whether to request ARC demos.\\\\nmetadata:\\\\n  hermes:\\\\n    tags: [arc, demonstrations, information-gain]\\\\n    related_skills: [arc-grid-reasoning, arc-demo-hypothesis-audit]\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# ARC demo-budget strategy\\\\n\\\\nUse when an ARC task charges for additional demonstrations or has a limited demo budget, particularly when the transformation's selector is unclear. The goal is not to exhaust the available examples; it is to gain enough discriminating evidence for a good decision while controlling request cost.\\\\n\\\\n## Procedure\\\\n\\\\n1. **Parse each pair into evidence.** Record exact changed cells and components, old/new colors, geometry, location, and unchanged alternatives. For object tasks use 4-connected components by default; test 8-connectivity only when diagonal contact could affect the rule.\\\\n2. **Maintain a small explicit hypothesis set.** Phrase candidates as testable selectors and transformations, e.g. \u201clargest non-background component,\u201d \u201cthe unique shape matching an exemplar,\u201d or \u201cthe component touching the guide line.\u201d Avoid vague accounts like \u201cthe interesting object changes.\u201d\\\\n3. **Audit positive and negative examples.** Each hypothesis must predict the selected object in every demo and must not predict edits to observed unchanged objects. Keep object selection and replacement-color mapping as separate questions.\\\\n4. **Plan before requesting.** Name the leading competing hypotheses and what feature of the next pair would distinguish them. If you cannot say how a possible result changes the ranking or action, the request has low information value.\\\\n5. **Track information gain and diminishing returns.** After each demo, note whether hypotheses were eliminated or merely accumulated another compatible case. Do not request again just because a cap remains. If several varied demos fail to isolate a common property, recognize that evidence may be saturated or underdetermined.\\\\n6. **Choose request vs submit by expected cost.** Request only when the next example could plausibly distinguish materially different rules and its expected value justifies its score cost versus a likely-wrong submission. If no useful request remains, make the best-supported deliberate choice rather than spending by default.\\\\n7. **Preserve uncertainty.** Reaching the demo cap does not validate a guess. If the selector remains ambiguous, do not claim a visually plausible candidate is established; alter only cells warranted by the strongest supported hypothesis.\\\\n8. **Honor the task interface.** When the environment requires exactly one JSON action, return exactly one action and no surrounding explanation.\\\\n\\\\n## Evidence ledger for changed-component tasks\\\\n\\\\n| Pair | Changed component | Before \u2192 after | Area / geometry | Position | Unchanged candidates |\\\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\\\n\\\\nInclude unchanged same-color components and similar shapes. This guards against largest/smallest-object and color-only fallacies. `arc-demo-hypothesis-audit` has broader component auditing guidance; this skill focuses on deciding when an additional example is worth its cost.\\\\n\\\\n## Continual-ARC lesson: task-fba999ff\\\\n\\\\nThree requested demonstrations sufficed to identify the transform: connect same-color cells sharing a row or column by recoloring only strict interior cells to 3; endpoints and other cells remain unchanged. Demo palettes/backgrounds varied, while connector color stayed 3. In the fresh 5x25 test, only the two 8-markers at row 1, columns 12 and 14 had a gap, so one cell changed. Adjacent aligned marker pairs create no interior. This example rewarded careful sparse test audit; no fourth demo was needed after pair 3 showed extensive row/column segment completion and the test's sole gap was unambiguous.\\\\n\\\\n## Continual-ARC note: task-b335a605 tiny horizontal-band case\\\\n\\\\nOne test's unchanged-grid guess was wrong: the 4\u00d74 input had full rows `[0,0,0,0]`, `[4,4,4,4]`, `[4,4,4,4]`, `[6,6,6,6]`. One demo showed a 7\u00d77 analog where only the leading all-zero row changed, becoming the color of the bottom row (4); all other full-width bands, including duplicate adjacent colors, stayed exactly as input. The accepted test changed its top row to 6 (bottom-row color) and preserved all other rows. This is one branch-specific example, not a universal rule for task-b335a605: the task ID also recurs with periodic diagonal and horizontal patterns. A targeted demo was worth its cost because no-op and leading-row-from-bottom hypotheses predicted different outputs. See `puzzles:arc-grid-reasoning` reference `task-b335a605-tiny-horizontal-bands.md`.\\\\n\\\\n## Continual-ARC note: task-b335a605 horizontal stripe residues\\\\n\\\\nA separate accepted 28\u00d728 instance with three nonzero full-width stripes (row 0 color 5, row 4 color 3, row 20 color 7) used stripe count `k=3` as the vertical period: each stripe's color fills its row residue modulo 3, yielding repeated `[5,3,7]`. Three demos supported this rule with different stripe counts/palettes; crucially, line colors are keyed by row modulo stripe count, not by numeric ordering. This is a separate recurrence branch from the tiny leading-row case and from diagonal periodic variants. See `puzzles:arc-grid-reasoning` reference `task-b335a605-horizontal-row-residue-pattern.md`.\\\\n\\\\n## Accepted recurrence note: task-2e5d2205 paired vertical edge runs (25\u00d76)\\\\n\\\\nA fresh test with no demos was accepted with zero wrong submissions, adding confirmation to the paired equal-length opposing-edge-run branch: background 6; color-3 vertical runs of length 4 at right edge rows 0\u20133 and left edge rows 19\u201322; color-7 markers in the left run at `(20,3),(21,4),(22,2)`. Markers became 4 and their rows filled with 7 inward toward the left edge (c1\u20132, c1\u20133, c1), preserving edge 3. Corresponding offsets in the opposite right-edge run filled rows 1\u20133 across c0\u20134, preserving right edge 3; row 0 and all unselected rows remained unchanged. See `arc-ray-projection/references/task-2e5d2205-edge-run-correspondence-25x6-accepted.md` for the exact accepted grid. This reinforces the paired equal-length-run rule only; do not apply it to geometrically different unresolved recurrences with the same ID.\\\\n\\\\n## Unsolved recurrence note: task-7c19c728 with mixed diagonal pairs\\\\n\\\\nA 25\u00d76 test was not solved after eight wrong submissions and two demos, despite two demos supporting diagonal same-color pair rays. Input (0-based) contained background 3; 4 at `(21,4),(22,3)`, 9 at `(22,2),(23,3)`, and 1 at `(23,2),(24,1)`. Demo 1 extended a 7-pair down-left and a 4-pair down-right; demo 2 extended a 6-pair down-left, painting through background until edge/blocker. Applying the apparent ray rule to the 9 and 4 pairs predicted 9 at `(20,0),(21,1),(24,4)` and 4 at `(20,5)`. This was rejected, including after removing the mistaken extra 4 at `(21,5)`; no candidate was accepted. The 1 pair appeared blocked on one side and boundary-limited on the other, but its exact relevance is unresolved. Treat this as uncertainty/negative evidence, not a validated transformation or selector. For future instances with multiple mixed-color/overlapping pairs, request a targeted demo before committing; do not overinterpret binary rejections.\\\\n\\\\n## Continual-ARC recurrence note: task-b335a605 periodic diagonal-stripe completion\\\\n\\\\nA solved fresh instance (19\u00d719, zero wrong submissions) and four varied demos establish a diagonal-stripe periodic fill branch. Treat this separately from the task ID's horizontal stripe-residue and tiny horizontal-band branches: recurring IDs may present different transfo",
